{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 4,
  "estimated_minutes": 30,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The reviewer needs only the sequence of the 2008–2012 device leases and protocol licenses, the later-issued patents, and ARPwave's reliance on the forum clauses to resist dismissal for improper venue."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "No understanding of electrotherapy technology or patent claim construction is needed; the relevant distinction is between infringement claims independent of the agreements and claims enforcing contractual duties."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The scope of 'enforcement' and 'relating to' requires comparing the forum clauses with payment, return, confidentiality, and noncompetition obligations across the three agreements."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Lines 41–49 expressly explain the scope rulings, and line 67 plainly separates the jury-waiver language from the forum provision, allowing the reviewer to set aside the nonsignatory and pendent-venue rulings."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "d05 and d06 address the same 'relating to' language through overlapping lexical and whole-contract reasoning, while d01–d04 repeat the same construction in separate lease and license provisions."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d06 — The reviewer must connect the 2012 clause's reference to related agreements with the substantive content of all three agreements and understand why a shared device history does not make the infringement claims contract-dependent. This also requires distinguishing that contextual application from d05's construction of 'relating to.'",
  "reason": "A general litigation reviewer can handle this case without patent or medical-device expertise. The main work is comparing the narrower 'enforcement' wording with the 2012 'relating to' wording and linking each to the contractual duties actually implicated by the claims. The reviewer should preserve the distinction between d05's lexical issue and d06's application across the agreements, rather than treating them as wholly separate disputes. The court explains its constructions directly, so targeted reading of the forum provisions, relevant substantive obligations, and scope analysis should produce confident understanding."
}
